 ## Overview
-Ray Marching with Reflections extends distance-field ray marching by continuing the ray path after a surface intersection to simulate specular reflections, effectively transitioning from raycasting to raytracing [S6, unofficial]. Unlike standard sphere tracing which terminates at the first hit, this method recursively or iteratively casts new rays in the reflected direction, attenuating their contribution based on material reflectivity [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from [Ray Marching with Refraction and Beer's Law Absorption](1947-ray-marching-with-refraction-and-beer-s-law-absorption.md) by preserving the ray within the same medium (no index of refraction change) and from [Approximate Refraction](3072-approximate-refraction.md) by providing an exact geometric reflection rather than a texture-coordinate offset [S1, unofficial].
